※※ Walking a tuple at compile time, and choosing by type.
※※
※※ A tuple's members are of whatever types they were written with, so a loop
※※ over one cannot be one body run again: the name would have to be of one type
※※ and there is no one type.  `comptime foreach` writes the body out instead --
※※ once per member, each with the name standing for that member and of that
※※ member's type.
※※
※※ `comptime if` then asks which type this turn's name is of.  `⎕typeof`
※※ answers a type, and a type is not a value: there is nothing for one to be at
※※ run time, which is why it may stand only in a question the compiler settles.
※※ What comes out of the whole of this is five additions in a row -- no loop, no
※※ counter, and no test of any kind.
※※
※※ The program adds each member to a different running total according to its
※※ type and checks the three totals afterwards.  It exits with 0 where they are
※※ what they should be, and with the number of the check that failed otherwise.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ five members, of three different types
    let values: 〈u8, u16, u32, u8, u16〉 = 〈1u8, 2u16, 3u32, 4u8, 5u16〉

    ※ one total per type, all of them outside the loop
    let bytes: mut u8 = 0u8
    let halves: mut u16 = 0u16
    let words: mut u32 = 0u32

    comptime foreach v := values:
        comptime if ⎕typeof(v) = u8:
            bytes ← bytes + v
        comptime elif ⎕typeof(v) = u16:
            halves ← halves + v
        comptime elif ⎕typeof(v) = u32:
            words ← words + v
        else:
            ※ nothing else can reach here, every member being one of the three
            words ← words

    if bytes ≠ 5u8:
        1u6
    elif halves ≠ 7u16:
        2u6
    elif words ≠ 3u32:
        3u6
    else:
        0u6
